# K-NRM Feature Diagnosis

Status: concluded on 2026-09-07.

## Objective

Determine whether the BGE-M3 K-NRM result is limited by absent token-level signal, the 11-dimensional kernel pooling representation, document-length leakage, or the downstream linear classifier.

This diagnosis reuses the materialized K-NRM features. It does not run BGE-M3 and does not train a classifier.

## Reproduce

```bash { name=diagnose-knrm-features }
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/ml"
.venv/bin/python scripts/diagnose_knrm_features.py
```

The detailed machine-readable result and plots are written to:

```text
artifacts/eval__knrm_feature_diagnosis.json
data/analysis/knrm_diagnosis/class_distributions.png
data/analysis/knrm_diagnosis/kernel_correlation.png
```

## Evaluation Contract

- All 31,333 outer-test rows from the three graph-disjoint folds are included.
- Each fold's standardizer and length nuisance regression are fitted on that fold's outer-train rows only.
- Held-out neighborhoods contain outer-train points only.
- The length-only control uses resume and JD token counts without semantic features.
- The residual control removes linear effects of raw and log resume/JD token counts.

## Marginal Class Signal

| Kernel | Center μ | Class η² | Mutual information |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1.0 | 0.0053 | 0.1283 |
| 1 | 0.9 | 0.0308 | 0.0401 |
| 2 | 0.7 | 0.0106 | 0.0189 |
| 3 | 0.5 | 0.0115 | 0.0166 |
| 4 | 0.3 | 0.0044 | 0.0128 |
| 5 | 0.1 | 0.0001 | 0.0065 |
| 6 | −0.1 | 0.0907 | 0.0568 |
| 7 | −0.3 | 0.0260 | 0.0127 |
| 8 | −0.5 | 0.0137 | 0.0234 |
| 9 | −0.7 | 0.0053 | 0.1467 |
| 10 | −0.9 | 0.0053 | 0.1251 |

Kernel 6 has the largest marginal class effect at η² = 0.0907. No individual kernel cleanly separates the three labels: every class has broad, overlapping interquartile ranges in every kernel.

The high mutual information of kernels 0, 9, and 10 is not evidence of useful semantic matching by those kernels. Those outputs are almost entirely determined by JD token count, which itself has dataset-level association with the labels.

## Redundancy And Length

| Measurement | Result |
| --- | ---: |
| Kernel pairs with absolute Pearson ρ ≥ 0.95 | 28 of 55 |
| Effective dimension of the 11×11 correlation matrix | 1.243 |
| Effective dimension after length residualization | 3.201 |
| Maximum absolute resume-length Spearman ρ | 0.145 |
| Maximum absolute JD-length Spearman ρ | 1.000 |

Kernels 0 and 10 are exactly correlated. Kernels 0, 9, and 10 are effectively duplicate floor-saturation channels. For these centers, almost all token similarities miss the Gaussian support, the soft count reaches the configured minimum, and summing the same floor contribution over JD tokens turns the output into a JD-length measurement.

The same length dependence affects most remaining kernels because the current pooling equation sums once per JD token. Ten of the eleven kernels have absolute JD-length Spearman correlation above 0.90.

## Held-Out Local Signal

Ten-neighbor purity measures the fraction of outer-train neighbors sharing each outer-test row's label.

| Fold | Class-prior expectation | Length only | K-NRM | K-NRM after length removal |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.5792 | 0.5823 | 0.6430 | 0.6465 |
| 1 | 0.5823 | 0.5839 | 0.6531 | 0.6546 |
| 2 | 0.5762 | 0.5875 | 0.6434 | 0.6408 |

Length alone is near the class-prior expectation. K-NRM is consistently 5.6 to 7.1 percentage points above that control. Removing length leaves the result effectively unchanged, so the low-variance residual features contain genuine local class information.

Class-conditioned same-label neighbor fractions remain asymmetric:

| Fold | No Fit | Potential Fit | Good Fit |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.7925 | 0.2538 | 0.1751 |
| 1 | 0.7878 | 0.2889 | 0.2098 |
| 2 | 0.7984 | 0.2566 | 0.1903 |

This agrees with the classifier result: No Fit has a strong local neighborhood, while Potential Fit and Good Fit overlap substantially with other classes. Potential Fit is not represented as a clean intermediate cluster.

## Diagnosis

The BGE-M3 token matrices contain more class signal than a document-length baseline, so this experiment does not support rejecting BGE-M3.

The present K-NRM pooling is the primary measured bottleneck:

1. It collapses the full token interaction matrix into 11 highly redundant global histogram totals.
2. Its sum over JD tokens makes raw feature variance overwhelmingly length-dependent.
3. Floor saturation makes three kernels nearly or exactly duplicate length channels.
4. It discards where a match occurred and whether matching evidence covers distinct requirements.

The linear head is also constrained by the resulting representation, but these measurements do not establish that a nonlinear head alone would solve the problem. Local neighborhoods still mix Potential Fit and Good Fit after length removal.

## Decision

Do not spend the next experiment on EM, learned Gaussian centers, or a larger classifier over these same 11 pooled values. Those changes retain the measured information bottleneck and length-sensitive aggregation.

The next representation should retain multiple requirement-level interaction signals and normalize aggregation with respect to document length before classification. BGE-M3 can remain frozen while that pooling layer is replaced. This diagnosis narrows the next experiment; it does not yet select its exact architecture.
